Here is the full audit report.

---

## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `SerializerTests.cs`

**Summary: 4 Critical · 1 High · 2 Medium · 0 Low**

This suite is dominated by **self-referential / tautological assertions**. Five of eight tests choose inputs or assert values such that the operation under test is a no-op, meaning the test passes *regardless of whether the production code works correctly*. Coverage numbers will look healthy, but these tests provide almost zero defect-detection value.

---

### Critical Findings

---

**① Self-referential assertion — `NormalizeName_AlreadyNormalized_NoChange` (line 37)**

`NameNormalizer.Normalize` does `.Trim().ToLowerInvariant()`. The test feeds in `"alice"` — already trimmed and lowercase — then asserts `input == result`. The operation is a no-op on this input so the assertion is trivially true. If `Normalize` were `return name;`, the test would still pass.

```csharp
// BEFORE (tautological)
var name = "alice";
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize(name);
Assert.AreEqual(name, result);

// AFTER — test the actual transformation
[DataTestMethod]
[DataRow("  Alice ", "alice")]
[DataRow("BOB",      "bob")]
[DataRow(" Carol",   "carol")]
public void NormalizeName_TrimsAndLowercases(string input, string expected)
{
    Assert.AreEqual(expected, NameNormalizer.Normalize(input));
}
```

---

**② Self-referential assertion — `GetDisplayName_ReturnsFormattedName` (line 56)**

`GetDisplayName()` returns `Name` verbatim. The test asserts `user.Name == display`, which is `user.Name == user.Name` — the test asserts a field against itself through a trivial accessor. It can never fail.

```csharp
// BEFORE (field == field)
Assert.AreEqual(user.Name, display);

// AFTER — assert against an independent expected value
var user = new UserDto { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", display);
```

If `GetDisplayName` is *supposed* to format (the name says "Formatted"), add a test for the expected format, e.g. `"Alice (30)"`.

---

**③ Self-referential assertion — `UpperCase_ToUpper_Works` (line 72)**

Input is `"HELLO"` — already uppercase. `"HELLO".ToUpper()` is a no-op, so `Assert.AreEqual(input, result)` is trivially true. This test cannot detect a broken `ToUpper`.

```csharp
// BEFORE (no-op input)
var input = "HELLO";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// AFTER — use an input that actually requires transformation
var input = "hello";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual("HELLO", result);
```

---

**④ Self-referential assertion — `Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsSameInput` (line 64)**

`ValidateEmail` returns the input unchanged on success. Asserting `input == result` is trivially true by contract for any valid email. The test never exercises the *actual* behaviour of this method — rejecting invalid emails.

```csharp
// BEFORE (trivially true)
var input = "hello@world.com";
var result = Validator.ValidateEmail(input);
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// AFTER — test what the validator actually does
[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_ValidInput_ReturnsInput()
{
    Assert.AreEqual("a@b.com", Validator.ValidateEmail("a@b.com"));
}

[TestMethod]
[DataRow("")]
[DataRow("   ")]
[DataRow("no-at-sign")]
public void ValidateEmail_InvalidInput_Throws(string bad)
{
    Assert.ThrowsException<ArgumentException>(
        () => Validator.ValidateEmail(bad));
}
```

---

### High Finding

---

**⑤ Round-trip on lossy mapping — `MapToDto_MapBack_RoundTrip` (line 46)**

`Mapper.ToDto` drops `Id` and `Email`; `Mapper.ToEntity` drops `Age`. A round-trip assertion (`entity → dto → entity == entity`) is meaningless on a lossy mapping — it either fails (because `Id` goes 1→0) or, if the data is chosen to survive the loss, is tautological. Either way, the *transformation itself* (field renaming, default values) is never independently verified.

```csharp
// AFTER — verify each mapping direction independently
[TestMethod]
public void ToDto_MapsFieldsCorrectly()
{
    var entity = new UserEntity { Id = 1, FullName = "Alice Smith", Email = "a@b.com" };
    var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", dto.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, dto.Age); // Age is not on entity; expect default
}

[TestMethod]
public void ToEntity_MapsFieldsCorrectly()
{
    var dto = new UserDto { Name = "Alice Smith", Age = 30 };
    var entity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", entity.FullName);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, entity.Id);   // Id not on DTO
    Assert.AreEqual("", entity.Email); // Email not on DTO
}
```

---

### Medium Findings

| # | Anti-Pattern | Location | Issue | Fix |
